            <ArticleTitle>Everolimus-associated Acute Kidney Injury in Patients with Metastatic Breast Cancer.</ArticleTitle>
            <Pagination>
                <MedlinePgn>406-409</MedlinePgn>
            </Pagination>
            <ELocationID EIdType="doi" ValidYN="Y">10.4103/ijn.IJN_304_16</ELocationID>
            <Abstract>
                <AbstractText>Recently, everolimus (Evl) has been introduced in the management of hormone receptor-positive metastatic breast cancer, in combination with aromatase inhibitors. Evl-induced acute kidney injury has hitherto been described in other malignancies, especially renal cell cancer, but only once before in a patient with breast cancer. We describe two cases of Evl-associated nephrotoxicity in patients with breast cancer, one of whom underwent a renal biopsy showing acute tubular necrosis. Both our patients improved after withdrawal of the offending agent and have normal renal functions on follow-up.</AbstractText>
            </Abstract>
